# Idea loop: Mapping AAV capsid-receptor interactions to explain barrier transcytosis versus intracellular retention

Root question: Can molecular mapping of AAV capsid-receptor interactions on brain microvascular endothelial cells determine which AAV variants transcytose across the blood-brain barrier versus remain intracellular, and for which capsid-receptor binding signatures?

Expanded from a one-line seed (framer: NOT COMPUTE-BOUND, so the widener listed the options): option Option 1: Exhaustive capsid–receptor complex enumeration (docking and AlphaFold-Multimer) "Exhaustive capsid–receptor complex enumeration (docking and AlphaFold-Multimer)" chosen by the gate and pick rule (tie of 3 broken by seeded order) among 5, 3 of which passed the gate.

### Expansion screen

| Option | stakes | open | reshape | total | gate | narrowing | changes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Option 1: Exhaustive capsid–receptor complex enumeration (docking and AlphaFold-Multimer) Exhaustive capsid–receptor complex enumeration (docking and AlphaFold-Multimer) | 4 | 5 | 1 | 9 | ranked | This leaves aside everything after binding — endosomal sorting, lysosomal degradation, nuclear import — so the computed interface geometry stands in for the binding step alone, not for fate. Its answer still moves the root question because it either widens or closes the catalog of candidate signatures (Q3) against which the sufficiency claim (Q1) must be tested. |  |
| Option 2: Microsecond molecular dynamics of capsid–receptor–membrane assemblies Microsecond molecular dynamics of capsid–receptor–membrane assemblies | 3 | 1 | 3 | 4 | compute effect settled: no open compute quantity | This leaves aside the cell's sorting machinery — retromer/SNX tubule engagement, lysosomal routing — and treats simulated binding/unbinding and membrane partitioning as a stand-in for the trafficking decision. Its answer still moves the root question by testing whether any fate-relevant physics is recoverable from the binding step at all, which is exactly the sufficiency issue Q1 raises. |  |
| Option 3: Supervised sequence-to-transcytosis phenotype models Supervised sequence-to-transcytosis phenotype models | 4 | 5 | 3 | 9 | ranked | This leaves aside the mechanism of the sorting decision and treats a predicted transcytosis score as a stand-in for measured transwell/in vivo fate. Its answer still moves the root question because a predictor that generalizes would show which feature set carries fate information, i.e. how far Q3's signatures can be pushed toward Q1's sufficiency claim. |  |
| Option 4: Cryo-EM reconstruction of capsid–receptor complexes Cryo-EM reconstruction of capsid–receptor complexes | 3 | 1 | 3 | 4 | compute effect settled: no open compute quantity | This leaves aside affinity, kinetics, and post-binding sorting, so the static interface stands in for the whole binding event. Its answer still moves the root question because residue-level contact maps are the raw material from which any binding signature (Q3) is defined. |  |
| Option 5: Cross-species receptor–capsid compatibility scoring Cross-species receptor–capsid compatibility scoring | 4 | 5 | 1 | 9 | ranked | This leaves aside mouse-only biology such as Ly6a polymorphism underlying strain-specific PHP.B tropism (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31696742) and treats interface conservation as a stand-in for actual human transcytosis. Its answer still moves the root question because translatability is a necessary filter on any signature Q3 proposes for human use. |  |

- Option 2: Microsecond molecular dynamics of capsid–receptor–membrane assemblies: not ranked (compute effect settled: no open compute quantity)
- Option 4: Cryo-EM reconstruction of capsid–receptor complexes: not ranked (compute effect settled: no open compute quantity)
