## Screen (option-critic panel, medians; gate from src/screen.js)

| Option | stakes | open | reshape | total | gate | narrowing | changes |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| O Exhaustive capsid–receptor complex enumeration (docking and AlphaFold-Multimer) | 3 | 5 | 1 | 8 | ranked | This leaves aside everything after binding — endosomal sorting, lysosomal degradation, nuclear import — so the computed interface geometry stands in for the binding step alone, not for fate. Its answer still moves the root question because it either widens or closes the catalog of candidate signatures (Q3) against which the sufficiency claim (Q1) must be tested. |  |
| V1 Full-capsid, membrane-anchored receptor enumeration of the 3-fold GPI engagement motif | 3 | 5 | 1 | 8 | ranked | Leaves aside everything after binding — endosomal sorting, lysosomal degradation, nuclear import — so the computed interface geometry stands in only for the binding step. It still moves the root question because it decides whether the candidate signature catalog (Q3) survives when the capsid is modeled as it actually engages the receptor, which sets what Q1's sufficiency claim has to test. | Changes the system studied — full 60-mer capsid with a GPI-anchored receptor in a membrane patch at the 3-fold spike, instead of isolated AA587–594 peptides. This closes the named gap that a 'shared 3-fold motif' may partly reflect the peptide-competition setup, and it matters to the Gradinaru/APPRAISE programme, whose current pipeline is peptide-based, and to Voyager's capsid–receptor mechanism step, which reads real capsid engagement. |
| V2 Motif call judged against matched human Transwell transcytosis labels on conserved ALPL | 4 | 5 | 1 | 9 | ranked | Leaves aside post-binding fate beyond the barrier readout and any receptor without a matched transcytosis measurement, so the observable stands in for transcytosis rate across human endothelium rather than for in vivo brain transduction. It moves the root question because it converts Q1's sufficiency claim into a test on human-relevant pairs instead of mouse-restricted signatures. | Changes the comparison it is judged against — matched human Transwell transcytosis measurements built around Voyager's VCAP-102±ALPL versus AAV9 result, instead of scarce mixed-species phenotype labels. This closes the label-scarcity gap and the species gap (ALPL is conserved while LY6A/LY6C1 are mouse-restricted and 9P31/9P36 bind mouse but not primate CA4), and it targets Voyager's TRACER / ALPL NeuroShuttle mechanism-of-action step and Dyno's ALPL mechanism assignment. |
| V3 Motif calls emitted for Capsida NR001–NR005 and Lir Starfish binding-assay readouts | 4 | 5 | 1 | 9 | ranked | Leaves aside transcytosis and all post-binding fate — the observable is binding agreement plus interface geometry, not fate. It still moves the root question by narrowing the candidate signature catalog (Q3) to pairs that survive a human-receptor binding assay, which is the precondition for any sufficiency test (Q1). | Changes the programme it is aimed at — output is scored against Capsida's NR001–NR005 human BBB-receptor platform and Lir's Starfish docking pipeline instead of against phenotype labels. This closes the named gap that these two additional consumers were not assessed and that binding-only, human-relevant receptors are exactly what their assays read. |

